{
  "edition": {
    "edition_date": "2026-11-01",
    "title": "Rural Update - Oklahoma edition",
    "bluf": "Prioritize October RHC telehealth claims reconciliation and Medicaid financing exposure. CMS's non-behavioral distant-site billing change took effect October 1, 2026, with systems implementation October 5. Provider-tax statutory changes already have effective dates; CMS-2452-P proposes implementing rules, so do not treat all exposure as contingent on finalization. Oklahoma's published RHTP awards warrant project-level funding and sustainability review. Verify Oklahoma's GENEROUS execution and provider guidance before estimating pharmacy effects. HRSA's September RCORP awards and Oklahoma's behavioral health relocation program support review of referral capacity and recruitment opportunities; awards do not establish that services are already available.",
    "stories": [
      {
        "what_changed": "Continuing watch: CMS MM14468 and CR14468 require individual CPT/HCPCS codes instead of G2025, with appropriate revenue codes and telehealth modifiers, for non-behavioral RHC/FQHC distant-site telehealth services for dates of service on or after October 1, 2026. CMS lists October 5 as the systems implementation date. Behavioral health telehealth payment remains distinct. Review October remittances and denials to establish the actual local effect.",
        "why_it_matters": "The coding change creates a reconciliation priority for non-behavioral RHC telehealth claims. Clearinghouse acceptance, remittances and denial logs should establish the local cash effect; the cited guidance does not establish any hospital-specific loss.",
        "executive_question": "Have you reconciled October non-behavioral RHC telehealth claims, checked CPT/HCPCS and modifiers, and separated behavioral health services under their applicable payment rules?",
        "category": "Medicare payment / Revenue cycle",
        "id": "rhc-telehealth-billing-change",
        "title": "RHC/FQHC non-behavioral telehealth: reconcile October claims under the new billing codes",
        "source_ids": [
          "src-3236e8cccb97f0d6",
          "src-0a355f6c88371f14",
          "src-9315ed0d2cc3ed6e"
        ],
        "change": "Continuing",
        "policy_state": "Final"
      },
      {
        "what_changed": "Section 71115 of Public Law 119-21 establishes statutory provider-tax limits effective October 1, 2026, and a phase-down for expansion states beginning federal fiscal year 2028. CMS-2452-P, published in July, proposes implementing definitions, calculations and reporting requirements. Distinguish these proposed regulations from the statutory changes they implement.",
        "why_it_matters": "Do not assume statutory exposure waits for final regulations. Identify revenue supported by provider-tax-funded supplemental payments and seek OHCA guidance on Oklahoma's applicable tax classes and transition provisions before estimating facility-level effects.",
        "executive_question": "What portion of your Medicaid revenue depends on provider-tax-funded supplemental payments, and can you obtain OHCA confirmation of Oklahoma exposure?",
        "category": "Medicaid financing / State policy",
        "id": "medicaid-financing-cms-2452-p",
        "title": "CMS proposed rule (CMS‑2452‑P) on provider taxes creates material state‑finance uncertainty",
        "source_ids": [
          "src-299e421cf61e682c",
          "src-f99f0b6e98fff5e6"
        ],
        "change": "Continuing",
        "policy_state": "Proposed"
      },
      {
        "what_changed": "Oklahoma's September 30 RHTP progress report describes microgrant and regional awards, including projects involving SWOSU and Weatherford Regional Hospital, and identifies October 31 as the start of budget year two. The retained report does not establish the current year-two award, executed terms or completed services for an individual hospital.",
        "why_it_matters": "RHTP subawards can seed telehealth, workforce and transport projects that improve access, but these are time-limited federal funds. Without enforceable post-grant sustainability budgets, hospitals and clinics risk taking on new recurring operating costs when grant funding ends.",
        "executive_question": "For any RHTP subaward your organization participates in, is there a documented post-grant operating budget that accounts for staffing, maintenance, replacement capital and recurring revenue?",
        "category": "Grants / Operational sustainability",
        "id": "oklahoma-rhtp-subawards",
        "title": "Oklahoma RHTP moves from award design to state subawards and microgrants; post‑grant sustainability is the operational risk",
        "source_ids": [
          "src-db7e2228ed4f8b9c",
          "src-800eafbf3fe418ed",
          "src-86a66477b02cd1ef"
        ],
        "change": "Continuing",
        "policy_state": "Monitoring"
      },
      {
        "what_changed": "CMS announced broad national participation in the GENEROUS Medicaid drug-payment model and reported that most states had applied and many had signed participation agreements as of Sept 18, 2026.",
        "why_it_matters": "CMS describes supplemental rebates under manufacturer and state agreements. The retained national announcement does not establish Oklahoma execution, MCO instructions or provider-level pharmacy and 340B effects. Obtain state-specific evidence before changing a forecast; do not assume a particular passthrough requirement.",
        "executive_question": "Has OHCA provided written confirmation of executed GENEROUS participation, covered-product lists and MCO passthrough guidance that would affect pharmacy or 340B revenue?",
        "category": "Medicaid pharmacy / 340B",
        "id": "generous-medicaid-conditional",
        "title": "CMS GENEROUS Medicaid drug‑payment model advanced nationally; Oklahoma execution remains conditional",
        "source_ids": [
          "src-cc7888b1e0b22d4c"
        ],
        "change": "Continuing",
        "policy_state": "Announcement"
      },
      {
        "what_changed": "Continuing watch: HRSA announced $89.3 million in RCORP awards on September 25, 2026 to support rural substance-use prevention, treatment and recovery services across 45 states and one territory. This is an earlier funding announcement, not a newly announced November 1 award.",
        "why_it_matters": "The awards support service and workforce expansion, but an award does not establish that local capacity is already available. Verify nearby recipients, implementation timing and referral availability before changing ED transfer or outpatient referral pathways.",
        "executive_question": "Have you mapped local RCORP-funded treatment and recovery resources and updated ED/transfer and outpatient referral pathways accordingly?",
        "category": "Behavioral health / Capacity",
        "id": "rural-sud-rcorp-fy2026",
        "title": "HRSA FY2026 RCORP awards expand rural SUD treatment capacity",
        "source_ids": [
          "src-0f485a8b6532e177"
        ],
        "change": "Continuing",
        "policy_state": "Announcement"
      }
    ],
